WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.620
The first film ended with the brain turning to ketone bodies.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.980
Now follow the blood from an overnight fast through five weeks without food.

3
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.560
Glucose slips by only about 1 mM.

4
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.760
Ketones climb from about 0.1 mM to 7 or 8.

5
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.700
If glucose barely moves, who is paying for it?

6
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:29.913
At first the liver pays from savings: its glycogen, about 100 grams.

7
00:00:31.353 --> 00:00:33.913
Within a day or two, that store is mostly spent.

8
00:00:35.293 --> 00:00:37.973
After that, nearly all glucose must be made new.

9
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:43.943
Made from what? The fatty acids of fat cannot become glucose.

10
00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:49.723
Only fat's glycerol backbone can, about 15 to 20 grams a day.

11
00:00:51.023 --> 00:00:54.143
The main raw material left is your own muscle protein.

12
00:00:55.283 --> 00:00:58.623
Muscle protein is broken into amino acids.

13
00:00:59.963 --> 00:01:02.463
Alanine carries carbon and nitrogen to the liver.

14
00:01:03.623 --> 00:01:07.563
The carbon can become glucose; the nitrogen is dumped as urea.

15
00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:10.603
The kidney joins in more and more.

16
00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:15.303
By five weeks, it makes about as much glucose as the liver.

17
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:17.967
Now total the brain's bill.

18
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:23.347
Burning only glucose, it needs about 120 grams a day.

19
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:26.507
Glycerol covers 15 to 20.

20
00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:32.447
The rest would cost about 180 grams of muscle protein daily.

21
00:01:33.807 --> 00:01:38.267
At that rate, a third of an adult's protein would be gone in about two and a half

22
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:39.267
weeks.

23
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.150
Here is the whole idea.

24
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.430
Blood glucose stays nearly level.

25
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.130
Underneath, the body keeps changing who pays for it, and how much.

26
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.800
Ketones cut the brain's glucose need to about a third, roughly 40 grams a day.

27
00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.100
Does muscle's share of the bill fall to a third as well?

28
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:02.380
Or further?

29
00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:09.593
Further: to about a fifth.

30
00:02:10.813 --> 00:02:14.953
In these rough sums, glycerol still covers its 15 to 20 grams.

31
00:02:16.313 --> 00:02:18.153
So the whole cut comes off muscle's share.

32
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:25.477
At that smaller bill, the fat, about 75 days of energy, runs out before the protein

33
00:02:25.477 --> 00:02:29.177
does. These sums show direction and size only.

34
00:02:30.597 --> 00:02:35.377
Measured protein loss in long starvation runs lower, nearer 20 grams a day.

35
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.890
Back to five weeks.

36
00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.890
Blood glucose has barely moved.

37
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.190
Behind it, the brain runs about two-thirds on ketones.

38
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.290
The kidney makes about as much glucose as the liver.

39
00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.070
And muscle pays a fraction of its old bill.

40
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.320
So the steady number in the blood hides a shifting bill.

41
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.980
Glycogen pays first, then muscle protein.

42
00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.720
Then fat, through ketones, takes over most of the brain's fuel.
